  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nHigh pressure settle south tonight and should lead to some crisp \nmorning temperatures on Thursday. Look for morning lows from the mid \n40s in the lower spots to the lower 50s.\n\nReturn flow sets up through the day Thursday with southerly winds \nlikely gusting to 25 mph west of I-29. Highs will generally climb \ninto the 80s, with some 70s across parts of northwest IA and \nsouthwest MN.\n\nThe leading edge of a mid level wave will sneak into central SD \nlate Thursday night into Friday morning, resulting in increased low \nto mid level warm advection. While most of the area is capped due to \ndry air, a small wedge of increased moisture around 8000 feet may \nbring weak, 200-400 J/kg, elevated instability based around 8000 \nfeet. This could result in some ACCAS on Friday morning, with \nimpacts on the minor side.\n\nThis wave will deepen a bit and track just north of the area on \nFriday afternoon and evening, leading to a wedge of increased \ntemperatures in the low levels. This will lead to widespread highs \nin the mid 80s to lower 90s. The question then comes to what are the \nconvective chances as this wedge of warm air settle south and cooler \nair filters in behind on Friday night. The environment ahead of this \nfront will be fairly unstable, 2000-3000 J/kg, with about 40 knots \nof deep layer shear. The lower levels show a nice south to southwest \ninflow, suggesting that new updrafts may be on the southwest flank \nof any development. Given the instability and shear as well as the \npotential for discrete cellular development, all modes of severe \nweather will be possible, including golf ball sized hail, wind gusts \nto 70 mph and isolated tornadoes. The main negative will be the \npotential for a fairly strong capping inversion. This suggests that \nthere may be a very small window shortly before sunset to get any \nstrong updrafts going that could produce higher end severe weather.\n\nAs this system lifts east northeast Friday night, cooler and drier \nair will settle southward and bring a pleasant Saturday with highs \nin the 70s and dew points in the 45 to 55 degree range.\n\nA stronger system works onto the Northern Plains Sunday into early \nMonday and should bring a more widespread area of showers and \nthunderstorms to the region. The latest ensemble output from the \nGEFS, EC and Canadian all suggest about a 30-60 percent chance for \na half an inch or more of rainfall for much of the area with a \nslight lean towards the better chances near and north of I-90.\n\nWest to southwest flow aloft continues through mid week keeping the \nchances for showers and thunderstorms in the area during this time. \nWith slightly cooler air in place behind the Sunday system and the \npotential for clouds and precipitation, temperatures will be a bit \ncooler with highs in the 70s and lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s.",
